[{"id":"29721","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following is an example of synthetic indicator?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Methyl orange</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Phenolphthalein</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Acid-base indicators are classified as natural (e.g., litmus, turmeric, red cabbage extract) or synthetic (chemically manufactured in a laboratory). Methyl orange and Phenolphthalein are both synthetically prepared organic compounds used as pH indicators in acid-base titrations, making both I and II correct examples of synthetic indicators.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29722","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Coal Mines Authority Limited (CMAL) with headquarters at Calcutta, was set up by the Government in May, ______ to manage non-coking coal mines.</span></p>","option_1":"1969","option_2":"1971","option_3":"1973","option_4":"1977","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 1973.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Coal Mines Authority Limited (CMAL) was established by the Government of India in May 1973, with its headquarters in Calcutta, to manage and oversee the operations of nationalised non-coking coal mines, following the nationalisation of the coal industry which was carried out in stages during 1971-73.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29723","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The Sambhar Lake is in ______.</span></p>","option_1":"Rajasthan","option_2":"Punjab","option_3":"Gujarat","option_4":"Haryana","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Rajasthan.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Sambhar Lake, India's largest inland saline (salt water) lake, is located in Rajasthan, spread across the districts of Jaipur, Ajmer, and Nagaur. It is a significant source of salt production in India and is also a Ramsar wetland site, known for hosting large flocks of migratory flamingos.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29724","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">As the river approaches the sea, the speed of the flowing water decreases and the river begins to break up into a number of streams called ______.</span></p>","option_1":"distributary","option_2":"levee","option_3":"floodplain","option_4":"tributary","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - distributary.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A \"distributary\" is a stream that branches off from the main river channel and flows away from it, typically occurring near a river's mouth (delta) where the reduced speed of the water causes it to split into multiple smaller channels before reaching the sea. A \"tributary\", in contrast, is a stream that joins into a larger river; a \"levee\" is a raised embankment along a riverbank; and a \"floodplain\" is the flat land bordering a river that gets submerged during floods.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29725","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Tracheid is part of which plant tissue?</span></p>","option_1":"Xylem","option_2":"Phloem","option_3":"Cuticle","option_4":"Bark","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Xylem.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Tracheids are elongated, tapering, dead conducting cells with lignified walls that form a key structural component of Xylem tissue, which is responsible for the conduction of water and minerals from roots to other parts of the plant, along with providing mechanical support. Phloem, in contrast, transports food (organic nutrients), while cuticle and bark are protective outer coverings, not conducting tissues.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29726","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Who killed the last Shunga ruler and founded the Kanva dynasty?</span></p>","option_1":"Vasudeva","option_2":"Agnimitra","option_3":"Kalashoka","option_4":"Pushyamitra","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Vasudeva.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Vasudeva (Vasudeva Kanva), a Brahmin minister of the last Shunga ruler Devabhuti, killed him around 73 BCE and founded the Kanva dynasty, which briefly ruled parts of the Magadha region after the decline of the Shungas. Pushyamitra Shunga was the founder of the Shunga dynasty itself (not its successor), Agnimitra was his son and successor, and Kalashoka belonged to the earlier Shishunaga dynasty.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29727","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">'Kuki' is a folk dance of which of the following Indian states?</span></p>","option_1":"Gujarat","option_2":"Karnataka","option_3":"Telangana","option_4":"Nagaland","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Nagaland.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Kuki' is a traditional folk dance associated with the Kuki tribal community, predominantly found in Nagaland and neighbouring North-Eastern states such as Manipur, typically performed during festive and harvest occasions.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29728","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">'Chamfuli', a popular dance performed by men and women in pairs, belongs to which Indian state/Union Territory?</span></p>","option_1":"Assam","option_2":"Lakshadweep","option_3":"Tamil Nadu","option_4":"Uttarakhand","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Uttarakhand.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Chamfuli' (also known as Chaunfla) is a popular folk dance of Uttarakhand, traditionally performed by men and women in pairs, often expressing themes of love and romance, and forming part of the region's rich Garhwali and Kumaoni folk dance traditions.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29729","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Where is the 'International Mango Festival' primarily celebrated in India?</span></p>","option_1":"Delhi","option_2":"Shimla","option_3":"Dispur","option_4":"Mysore","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Delhi.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The International Mango Festival is an annual event primarily celebrated in Delhi, organised to showcase hundreds of mango varieties from across India, along with cultural programmes, competitions, and mango-based culinary displays, aimed at promoting India's rich mango heritage.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29730","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The U R Rao Satellite Centre is in ______.</span></p>","option_1":"Chennai","option_2":"Bengaluru","option_3":"Kochi","option_4":"Lucknow","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Bengaluru.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The U R Rao Satellite Centre (URSC), formerly known as the ISRO Satellite Centre (ISAC), is located in Bengaluru, Karnataka. It is the lead centre of ISRO responsible for the design, development, fabrication, and testing of Indian satellites, and was renamed in honour of former ISRO Chairman Dr. U R Rao.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29731","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following planets appears blue from outer space because two-thirds of its surface is covered by water?</span></p>","option_1":"Earth","option_2":"Mercury","option_3":"Venus","option_4":"Uranus","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Earth.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Earth is often called the \"Blue Planet\" because approximately 71% (roughly two-thirds) of its surface is covered by oceans and other water bodies, giving it a distinctive blue appearance when viewed from outer space. Mercury and Venus have no surface water, and Uranus appears blue-green due to methane in its atmosphere, not due to surface water.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29732","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">'Jhumti' song is associated with which Indian state/UT?</span></p>","option_1":"Jharkhand","option_2":"Andhra Pradesh","option_3":"Lakshadweep","option_4":"Andaman and Nicobar","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Jharkhand.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Jhumti' (or Jhumair) is a traditional folk song/dance form associated with Jharkhand, commonly performed by tribal communities during harvest festivals and social celebrations, often accompanied by instruments like the mandar and nagara.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29733","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">India has about ______ villages according to the Census 2011.</span></p>","option_1":"340867","option_2":"140867","option_3":"640867","option_4":"840867","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 640867.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per Census 2011, India has approximately 640,867 villages, reflecting the country's predominantly rural settlement pattern, with a large majority of the population residing in these villages across various states and union territories.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29734","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following acts gave the government enormous powers and allowed detention of political prisoners without trial for two years?</span></p>","option_1":"Indian Councils Act 1861","option_2":"Indian Councils Act 1892","option_3":"Morley-Minto act","option_4":"Rowlatt Act","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Rowlatt Act.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Rowlatt Act of 1919 (formally the Anarchical and Revolutionary Crimes Act) granted the colonial government sweeping powers to arrest and detain individuals suspected of terrorism/sedition without trial for up to two years, and curtailed civil liberties such as the right to a fair trial. It sparked widespread protests in India, including the tragic Jallianwala Bagh massacre. The Indian Councils Acts (1861, 1892) and the Morley-Minto reforms (1909) primarily dealt with legislative council reforms, not detention powers.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29735","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The child sex ratio in India was ______ females per thousand males in 2011 census.</span></p>","option_1":"918","option_2":"980","option_3":"960","option_4":"987","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 918.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per Census 2011, the child sex ratio (0-6 years age group) in India stood at 918 females per thousand males, reflecting a decline compared to Census 2001 (927), and remains a matter of significant concern regarding gender-selective practices in the country.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29736","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which Indian initiative uses AI to provide internet and digital services in multiple Indian languages?</span></p>","option_1":"Umang","option_2":"DigiYatra","option_3":"Aarogya Setu","option_4":"BHASHINI","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - BHASHINI.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>BHASHINI (Bhasha Interface for India), launched under the National Language Translation Mission, is an AI-driven initiative aimed at providing internet and digital services in multiple Indian languages, using AI-based language translation and speech recognition technologies to bridge language barriers. Umang is a unified government services app, DigiYatra is for paperless air travel, and Aarogya Setu was a COVID-19 contact tracing app — none of which focus on multilingual AI-based services.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29737","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">What causes water droplets to form on the outer surface of a glass containing ice-cold water?</span></p>","option_1":"The ice-cold water is evaporating.","option_2":"Water vapour in the air condenses upon contact with the cold glass.","option_3":"The glass is absorbing moisture from the air.","option_4":"The glass is heating up the surrounding air.","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Water vapour in the air condenses upon contact with the cold glass.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>When warm, humid air comes into contact with the cold outer surface of a glass containing ice-cold water, the air near the glass is cooled below its dew point, causing the water vapour present in it to condense into tiny liquid droplets on the surface — this is the phenomenon of condensation, not evaporation, absorption, or heating.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29738","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Legionnaires' disease, which caused an outbreak in New York in August 2025, is a form of which illness?</span></p>","option_1":"Asthma","option_2":"Pneumonia","option_3":"Tuberculosis","option_4":"Influenza","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Pneumonia.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Legionnaires' disease is a severe form of bacterial pneumonia caused by Legionella bacteria, typically contracted by inhaling contaminated water droplets/mist (e.g., from cooling towers or air-conditioning systems). An outbreak of this disease was reported in New York in August 2025, linked to contaminated water systems in the affected area.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29739","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following countries is NOT part of Mineral Security Partnership?</span></p>","option_1":"Australia","option_2":"Canada","option_3":"India","option_4":"China","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - China.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Mineral Security Partnership (MSP), launched by the United States, is a collaboration of like-minded countries (including Australia, Canada, India, and others) aimed at securing critical mineral supply chains for clean energy and other technologies. China, despite being a dominant global player in critical mineral processing, is notably NOT a member of this US-led partnership.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29740","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Where were the 2006 Winter Olympics held?</span></p>","option_1":"Turin","option_2":"Moscow","option_3":"Zurich","option_4":"Dras","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Turin.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The 2006 Winter Olympics (XX Olympic Winter Games) were held in Turin (Torino), Italy. Moscow and Zurich have not hosted the Winter Olympics, and Dras (in Ladakh, India) is known for hosting winter sports events but not the Olympics.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29741","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">'The Race of My Life' is the autobiography of which Indian athlete?</span></p>","option_1":"P. T. Usha","option_2":"Milkha Singh","option_3":"Neeraj Chopra","option_4":"Abhinav Bindra","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Milkha Singh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'The Race of My Life' is the autobiography of legendary Indian sprinter Milkha Singh, popularly known as \"The Flying Sikh\", co-authored with his daughter Sonia Sanwalka, chronicling his remarkable journey from a refugee during Partition to becoming one of India's greatest athletes.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29742","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In the context of LPG reforms in India, the opening of the economy has led to a rapid _____.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. increase in the foreign direct investment</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. decrease in the foreign exchange reserve</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Only I.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The LPG (Liberalisation, Privatisation, Globalisation) reforms of 1991 opened up the Indian economy to foreign trade and investment, leading to a substantial and rapid increase in foreign direct investment (FDI) inflows into the country (Statement I correct). Contrary to Statement II, the reforms actually led to a significant increase (not decrease) in India's foreign exchange reserves over time, as the economy became more integrated with global markets, making Statement II incorrect.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29743","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Number of Players in handball on the court excluding goalkeeper in each team is _____.</span></p>","option_1":"6","option_2":"10","option_3":"8","option_4":"9","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 6.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In a standard handball match, each team fields 7 players on the court, comprising 1 goalkeeper and 6 outfield (court) players. Hence, excluding the goalkeeper, there are 6 players from each team on the court at a time.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29744","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following institutions was established by Rabindranath Tagore?</span></p>","option_1":"Presidency College","option_2":"Santiniketan","option_3":"Fort William College","option_4":"Hindu College","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Santiniketan.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Rabindranath Tagore established Santiniketan in West Bengal, which later grew into Visva-Bharati University in 1921, envisioned as a unique centre of learning blending Indian and global traditions, emphasising nature-based education. Presidency College, Fort William College, and Hindu College were established by the British colonial administration/other individuals, not by Tagore.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29745","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"774","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which revolutionary freedom fighter is popularly remembered as \"Shaheed-e-Azam\"?</span></p>","option_1":"Bhagat Singh","option_2":"Lala Lajpat Rai","option_3":"Chandrashekhar Azad","option_4":"Abbas Ali","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Bhagat Singh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span> </span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Bhagat Singh, the revolutionary freedom fighter executed by the British in 1931 for his role in the Lahore Conspiracy Case and the killing of British officer John Saunders, is popularly remembered as \"Shaheed-e-Azam\" (Great Martyr) for his sacrifice and role in India's independence movement.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""}]